Data verified. One concern: my segmenter in index.html — the active-set update logic at breakpoints. Let me re-check:

```js
breaks.forEach(function (p) {
  if (p > pos) { html.push(segment(text.slice(pos,p), active)); pos = p; }
  active = active.filter(s => s.end > p);
  spans.forEach(s => {
    if (s.start <= p && p < s.end && !active.some(a => a.cat===s.cat && a.end===s.end)) active.push(s);
  });
});
```

Issue: at the first breakpoint, pos=0 and p>0, so we push segment(0..p) with active=[] — correct since no span starts before first breakpoint (breakpoints include all starts). Then at each p, we first render up to p with old active, then update. A span starting exactly at p gets added after rendering. A span ending at p gets removed. Good.

But there's a subtle bug: `s.start <= p` — a span with start < p that was already active and end > p: it gets re-added check `!active.some(...)` — it's still in active after filter (end > p), and the some() matches (same cat, same end), so not duplicated. OK.

Overlapping spans of same category at same range: dedup via cat+end check. Fine.

One issue: a span whose start equals previous break but wasn't added? All starts are breakpoints, so any span with start <= p was added at its start breakpoint. But condition `s.start <= p && p < s.end` scans all spans every breakpoint — O(B*S). 13,823 spans total, per contract max maybe a few hundred; fine.

Edge: what if a span starts before the first breakpoint? Impossible since all starts are breakpoints. Good.

Another check: mark without background style for multi — CSS class .multi has striped gradient with !important. OK.

Node forEach on NodeList: `textEl.querySelectorAll("mark").forEach` — NodeList.forEach is supported in modern browsers. Fine.

scrollIntoViewIfNeeded is Safari/Chrome — guarded with if. Fine.

RTL trick for title ellipsis: works.

Now, can I actually test in a browser? No browser tool. I could do a basic sanity check with a headless approach — maybe not available. The JS is simple enough. Let me at least lint the inline script by extracting it and running node --check.

